nina (docs): Heads-up: I'm pushing a small docs PR to clarify how 'usage tiers' are calculated, add a short migration note for renamed panels, and include an SDK snippet for header fallbacks. Draft: https://github.com/redwood/docs/pull/1309

rahul (billing): Please add one line that tier transitions are prorated within a billing window. That's tripping up customers.

ivy (analytics): Also include the derived metric name 'effective_tier' used in dashboards and the new panel id grafana:panel.usage.tiers.2026 so alerts don't break.

dave (sdk): I'll drop a tiny Go snippet for reading both headers and falling back. Keep it one function.

nina (docs): Proposed migration blurb: "If saved Grafana panels 404 after the rename, search for grafana:panel.usage.tiers.2026 and update your links; rebind saved alerts to the new panel if needed." Thoughts?

rahul (billing): Good. Add a short example showing the prorate math: e.g., "If you spend 6 hours in tier B and 24 hours in tier A in a 30-day window, charges for tier B are prorated to the 6-hour portion." Keep it non-numeric heavy.

ivy (analytics): Note for dashboard readers: the 'burst_credits' value is cumulative and not an instantaneous rate. We'll add a tooltip but docs should warn people about interpretation.

dave (sdk): Go snippet:

```go
// headerFallback returns preferred header then legacy
func headerFallback(h http.Header, keys ...string) string {
    for _, k := range keys {
        if v := h.Get(k); v != "" {
            return v
        }
    }
    return ""
}
// usage: headerFallback(resp.Header, "X-RW-Rate", "X-Rate-Limit")
```

nina (docs): Nice and tiny, perfect. I'll add both snippets (JS + Go) and the compatibility table: old header -> new header, old panel id -> grafana:panel.usage.tiers.2026, action needed for Hosted/Dedicated.

